# Rad.FM Ops — secrets and configuration
#
# Copy to `.dev.vars` for local dev. `.dev.vars` is gitignored and must stay that way.
# For production:  bunx wrangler secret put <KEY>
#
# ┌─ NO LIVE VALUES ARE COMMITTED HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ─────────────────────────────────┐
# │ This file lists what is needed and where to get it. It never contains real credentials.     │
# │ A `.dev.vars.bak` full of production secrets was once committed to the backend repo; that   │
# │ is the mistake this file exists to not repeat.                                              │
# └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘


# =================================================================================================
# 1. CLOUDFLARE API TOKEN  — the one thing Patrick must create before work can start
# =================================================================================================
#
# Create at: dash.cloudflare.com -> My Profile -> API Tokens -> Create Token -> Custom Token
#
# Make a NEW token scoped to this dashboard. Do not reuse the backend's token, and do not use a
# Global API Key. If this internal tool is ever compromised, the blast radius should be "read some
# analytics", not "redeploy the Worker and repoint DNS".
#
#   REQUIRED (read-only — Phases 1 and 2):
#     Account : Workers Observability : Read      logs, errors, the 4xx blind spot
#     Account : Account Analytics     : Read      Analytics Engine SQL + GraphQL metrics
#     Account : Workers Scripts       : Read      deploy history
#
#   ADD LATER, ONLY IF THE DASHBOARD NEEDS TO ACT (Phase 3+):
#     Account : D1                    : Edit      ad-hoc SQL. Prefer routing through the backend.
#     Account : Workers KV Storage    : Edit      cache purge, IP denylist
#     Account : Workers R2 Storage    : Read      station artwork
#     Account : Workers Scripts       : Edit      ROLLBACK. Grant only when someone will use it.
#
# Two things that will otherwise waste an afternoon:
#   - The wrangler OAuth token in ~/.wrangler/ does NOT work against api.cloudflare.com/client/v4.
#     It returns `10000 Authentication error` no matter how fresh it is. You need a real API token.
#   - Start read-only. Adding a permission later is one click; discovering the dashboard could drop
#     a table is not.
#
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=


# =================================================================================================
# 2. BACKEND JWT  — LOCAL DEV ONLY
# =================================================================================================
#
# There is no separate admin login: "reuse the existing admin" resolved to reusing the existing user
# auth, so the dashboard carries the OPERATOR'S OWN Rad.FM JWT rather than holding a credential of
# its own. In the browser that token is pasted into the sidebar and lives in sessionStorage, so it
# dies with the tab.
#
# This variable is the local-dev convenience only: it lets you exercise /admin/* without pasting a
# token on every reload. Do NOT set it as a production secret — that would give every Access user
# the rights of whoever's token it is, which is exactly the attribution problem DEV_TKN_KEY has.
#
DEV_BACKEND_JWT=


# =================================================================================================
# 3. NON-SECRET CONFIG  — lives in wrangler.jsonc, not here
# =================================================================================================
#
# These are identifiers, not credentials, so they are committed in `wrangler.jsonc` under `vars`
# where they are reviewable:
#
#   CF_ACCOUNT_ID        49b85a65aa7b9cd658945400b972d2b7
#   BACKEND_ORIGIN       https://api.rad-fm.com
#   BACKEND_SCRIPT_NAME  rad-fm-backend   (the script Observability and GraphQL are queried for)
#   ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN   e.g. radfm.cloudflareaccess.com
#   ACCESS_AUD           Application Audience tag, from the Access app settings
#
# Access injects a signed JWT as the `cf-access-jwt-assertion` header, verified in worker/access.ts
# against the team JWKS. Verifying is not optional: Access protects the UI route, but anything that
# can reach the Worker directly bypasses it unless the origin checks the assertion itself.
#
# NOTE: while ACCESS_AUD is still the REPLACE_WITH_APPLICATION_AUD placeholder, the Worker runs its
# local-dev bypass and every /api route is UNAUTHENTICATED. That is keyed on the placeholder rather
# than on NODE_ENV so that configuring Access closes it automatically — but it does mean an
# half-configured deploy is an open one. Set both values before the Worker is reachable.


# =================================================================================================
# 4. WHAT THIS WORKER MUST NEVER HOLD
# =================================================================================================
#
# Under the architecture in README.md §2 the ops dashboard has no use for any of these. Every one
# copied in is a new place to leak from, in a tool that by design has broad read access.
#
#   JWT_SECRET              Admin identity is resolved by the BACKEND from the user's existing JWT.
#                           If the dashboard could mint tokens, an ops-tool compromise would become
#                           an auth compromise for every user.
#   OPENAI_API_KEY          Artwork generation. Backend only.
#   GROQ_API_KEY            DJ text. Backend only.
#   APPLE_MUSIC_DEV_TOKEN   Music proxy. Backend only.
#   POSTMARK_TOKEN          Transactional email — i.e. the ability to send mail as Rad.FM.
#   RC_WEB_API_KEY          RevenueCat. Entitlement checks go through the backend so they stay
#                           consistent with the live-confirmation logic that fixed the stale-cache
#                           incident.
#   DEV_TKN_KEY             The legacy shared admin key. Should be RETIRED as part of this project,
#                           not propagated into a second repo. See README.md §3.
#   REVIEW_ACCOUNT_OTP      App Store reviewer fixed OTP.
